<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cavtat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Epidaurum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cavtat is a small town on the Adriatic coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, on the site of the Greek and Roman town of Epidaurum. According to tradition, refugees from Epidaurum founded Ragusa (Dubrovnik) after the town was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> in the seventh century. Later part of the Republic of Ragusa, it is known for the Račić family mausoleum by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mestrovic">Ivan Meštrović</a> and as the birthplace of the painter Vlaho Bukovac.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cavtat.jpg" alt="Cavtat" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cavtat.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
